---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { url } from "../data/site";
import type { Metadata } from "../types";

const meta: Metadata = {
  title: "Teaching Philosophy",
  description:
    "A self-taught trombonist’s approach to lessons: wide-ranging repertoire, technique tied to real music, and making music less daunting.",
};
---

<Layout {meta}>
  <h1>Teaching Philosophy</h1>

  <p>
    Growing up without private lessons and being a result of the LAUSD public school system, I am largely self-taught. I
    was given the advice in middle school to play as wide a variety of music as possible and I really took that to
    heart. At lunch and nutrition, you always could find me in the band room practicing, or more accurately, fooling
    around and sight reading anything I could get my hands on.
  </p>

  <p>
    In middle school, I would scrounge around the music cabinets and in high school I would search for music on the
    internet. From playing out of the real book to reading method books front to back like a chapter book, I’ve read a
    lot, and I pride myself on being a player that can do a bit of everything. A goal of mine is to show my students
    everything the trombone has to offer, while also catering lessons towards their interests.
  </p>

  <p>
    However in order to make music, I believe in consistent practice in technique. Technique can often feel overwhelming
    and monotonous at times and I understand. To avoid this, I always make sure technique is relevant for actual music,
    either currently being worked on or for a goal piece in the future. Fundamentals give players the freedom and
    flexibility to play the music they want to play.
  </p>

  <p>
    From being a section leader/principal trombonist in middle school, high school, and college, I can teach many
    different skill levels. I understand that music is highly abstract to most people, but with thoughtful and precise
    analogies, I aim to create good habits in a natural and memorable way. For examples of my teaching style, feel free
    to look at my teaching packets in the <a href={url("/packets/")}>free resources</a> tab, which I often use with my
    high school students to teach major ideas.
  </p>

  <p class="lead">
    I always want to make music less daunting and more accessible, and I hope my website reflects that.
  </p>
</Layout>
